    <body>
      <sec id="article-18081.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Acute vision loss, ocular trauma, headache, and suspected intracranial hypertension are time-sensitive emergency presentations. Etiologies include retinal detachment, vitreous hemorrhage, central retinal artery occlusion, lens dislocation, retrobulbar hematoma, intraocular foreign bodies, and elevated intracranial pressure. Risk factors include trauma, degenerative vitreous changes, vascular disease, diabetes mellitus, and connective tissue disorders. Patients may present with photopsias, floaters, scotomas, sudden painless vision loss, visual field deficits, orbital pain, or neurologic symptoms. Examination is often limited by swelling, corneal opacity, or poor visualization. Point-of-care ocular ultrasound enables rapid bedside evaluation when direct visualization is inadequate. Indications include suspected retinal or vitreous pathology, lens dislocation, intraocular foreign body, retrobulbar hematoma, and elevated intracranial pressure; suspected globe rupture is a contraindication. When performed by trained clinicians, diagnostic accuracy is high. Mastery of technique and interpretation supports timely diagnosis, clinical decision-making, and interprofessional care.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Evaluate patients for suitability for ocular ultrasound based on clinical presentation and risk factors.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Interpret point-of-care ultrasound findings to diagnose acute vision-threatening pathology.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Determine patient suitability for urgent escalation of care after ocular ultrasound based on identified pathologic features and clinical context.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with members of the interprofessional team, including specialists such as emergency medicine physicians, ophthalmologists, and radiologists, to provide efficient, comprehensive, and coordinated care for individuals undergoing ocular ultrasound.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=18081&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=18081">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-18081.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Point-of-care ocular ultrasound (POCUS) is a valuable adjunct for evaluating patients in the emergency department who present with acute vision loss, ocular trauma, headache, or suspected elevated intracranial pressure (ICP). A comprehensive ophthalmologic assessment is essential and should include an external examination, slit-lamp evaluation, intraocular pressure (IOP) measurement, and fluorescein staining. However, these techniques may be limited in certain clinical situations.</p>
        <p>Ocular ultrasound provides rapid bedside imaging of intraocular and orbital structures and can offer important diagnostic information when direct visualization is difficult or incomplete&#x000a0;(see&#x000a0;<bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Ultrasound of the Right Globe). When used in conjunction with the standard ocular examination, POCUS aids in the timely identification of vision- and life-threatening conditions, facilitating prompt management and specialist consultation.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-18081.s3" sec-type="Anatomy and Physiology">
        <title>Anatomy and Physiology</title>
        <p>A comprehensive understanding of the anatomy of the globe and surrounding structures is critical for providers performing POCUS (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Anatomical Diagram and Sonographic&#x000a0;Image&#x000a0;of the Eye). Fat and muscle within the orbital cavity surround the globe, composed of the sclera, uvea, and retina, which enclose the aqueous and vitreous humor.</p>
        <p>The sclera constitutes the outermost layer of the eye and functions as a protective barrier surrounding the globe.&#x000a0;Posteriorly, the sclera is contiguous with the dural sheath of the optic nerve&#x000a0;(cranial nerve II).</p>
        <p>The uvea lies deep to the sclera and consists of the iris, ciliary body, and choroid. The iris is a pigmented ring with a central opening, the pupil, and attaches peripherally to the ciliary body, separating the anterior and posterior chambers of the globe. The lens lies immediately posterior to the iris. The ciliary body links the iris to the ciliary muscle, while the choroid forms a vascular layer between the sclera and the retinal pigment epithelium. Zonular fibers connect the ciliary body to the lens.</p>
        <p>Critical ocular vasculature includes the central retinal artery (CRA), originating from the ophthalmic artery, and the central retinal vein (CRV). The optic nerve transmits visual information from the retina to the brain. The&#x000a0;CRA and CRV traverse the center of the optic nerve, enclosed within the optic nerve sheath. The optic nerve sheath is contiguous with the dura mater and contains cerebrospinal fluid. The sheath communicates directly with the intracranial subarachnoid space.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>Using ultrasound, the cornea&#x000a0;may be visualized as a thin hyperechoic curvilinear structure and is the most anterior portion of the eye, located just behind the eyelid. The anterior chamber appears as the anechoic space between the cornea and the uvea. The iris and ciliary body appear on ultrasound as a hyperechoic line at the posterior margin of the anterior chamber, with a central anechoic opening corresponding to the pupil. The lens is visualized immediately posterior to the iris as a circular structure with hyperechoic edges and an anechoic center.</p>
        <p>The vitreous chamber constitutes the majority of the globe and appears as a large anechoic sphere. The retina lies along the posterior aspect of the globe. The optic nerve appears as a hypoechoic linear structure extending posteriorly from the retina at the back of the eye. The optic disc normally lies flush with the retina. Slight elevation into the posterior chamber may be observed with increased ICP. The CRA&#x000a0;and CRV course along the optic nerve and&#x000a0;may be identified with the application of color flow Doppler.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-18081.s4" sec-type="Indications">
        <title>Indications</title>
        <p>
<bold>Vision Loss</bold>
</p>
        <p>Retinal and vitreous detachment often present with complaints of flashes, floaters, or partial or complete vision loss. Retinal detachment appears on ultrasound as a mobile hyperechoic membrane or flap, frequently demonstrating serpentine motion with ocular movement. Retinal detachments remain tethered to the optic nerve and the ora serrata.</p>
        <p>Clinical assessment should distinguish macula-on from macula-off retinal detachment. The macula lies temporal to the optic nerve and is responsible for high visual acuity. Preservation of macular attachment (macula-on status) indicates potential for vision preservation with immediate treatment. Detachment involving the macula (macula-off status) indicates significant visual compromise, with no added benefit of emergent intervention compared with urgent intervention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r2">[2]</xref> Posterior vitreous detachment demonstrates lower echogenicity and lacks tethering to the optic disc (see&#x000a0;<bold>Video</bold>.&#x000a0;Dynamic Ultrasonography of Retinal Detachment).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>Vitreous hemorrhage appears as heterogeneous echogenic material within the globe that swirls with ocular movement, often described as the &#x0201c;snow globe&#x0201d; effect. Vitreous hemorrhage may occur in isolation or in association with retinal or vitreous detachment or trauma. Visualization improves with increased gain, as findings may be subtle under standard settings. Subacute hemorrhage may appear less echogenic as blood products become more hypoechoic, resulting in inconclusive ultrasound findings. Alternative diagnostic methods, such as direct ophthalmoscopy, may be required in cases of persistent clinical suspicion (see&#x000a0;<bold>Video</bold>.&#x000a0;Dynamic Ultrasonography of Vitreous Hemorrhage).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>CRA&#x000a0;occlusion (CRAO) presents with painless acute vision loss. Case reports describe CRAO on ultrasound as a hyperechoic focus posterior to the globe, with absence of arterial flow on color Doppler confirmation (see&#x000a0;<bold>Image</bold>. Central Retinal Artery Occlusion).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>Lens dislocation (ectopia lentis)&#x000a0;is marked by&#x000a0;the displacement of the lens from its normal position posterior to the iris. Ultrasound may demonstrate the lens within the vitreous chamber, separate from its expected anatomic location. Trauma is a common etiology.&#x000a0;However, minimal or absent trauma may be observed in patients with connective tissue disorders.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>Vitreous syneresis represents a degenerative process involving breakdown of the normally uniform gelatinous vitreous matrix. Progressive reorganization and contraction of vitreous structural components lead to the separation of liquid portions, the formation of small fluid-filled spaces, and the aggregation of residual solid elements. Clinical manifestations are typically benign but may include visual disturbances such as floaters or limited posterior vitreous detachment. On ultrasound, vitreous syneresis may appear as a mobile web of hyperechoic echoes within the vitreous chamber and may be mistaken for vitreous hemorrhage or other similar-appearing pathology.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Headache</bold>
</p>
        <p>Increased ICP and papilledema are important considerations in patients presenting with headache (see&#x000a0;<bold>Image</bold>. Papilledema on Funduscopy). The optic nerve is contained within a dural sheath that communicates directly with the subarachnoid space. ICP&#x000a0;elevation leads to optic nerve sheath expansion, followed by posterior protrusion of the optic disc into the vitreous chamber. This finding is known as papilledema and&#x000a0;may be visualized on ultrasound (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Papilledema on Ultrasound). Studies have demonstrated a correlation between increased&#x000a0;ICP and optic nerve sheath diameter (ONSD) greater than 5 mm when measured 3 mm posterior to the retina. Direct ultrasound visualization of papilledema may demonstrate optic disc elevation greater than 0.6 mm above the retina.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Trauma</bold>
</p>
        <p>Intraocular foreign bodies (IOFBs) appear on ultrasound as echogenic structures within the globe, often composed of materials such as metal, glass, or wood. Identification of an IOFB should raise concern for associated globe rupture. Suspected globe rupture&#x000a0;is a contraindication to ocular ultrasound, and further examination should be discontinued to avoid application of pressure to the injured eye.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>Rupture is characterized by disruption of the integrity of the globe. High clinical suspicion for globe perforation or rupture generally precludes ocular ultrasound. When imaging is inadvertently obtained, findings may include an irregular globe contour or a heterogeneous intraocular collection of blood, vitreous, and hematoma within the posterior chamber.</p>
        <p>Retrobulbar hematoma is the accumulation of blood within the retrobulbar space and constitutes a vision-threatening emergency, often associated with trauma. Progressive hemorrhage may elevate IOP and compress the optic nerve. On ultrasound, a retrobulbar hematoma may appear as an anechoic or heterogeneous collection posterior to the globe. Increasing pressure may lead to deformation of the posterior globe, producing the characteristic &#x0201c;guitar pick sign,&#x0201d; reflecting conical distortion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r18">[18]</xref></p>
        <p>Ocular ultrasound can assist in evaluating ICP elevation by measuring ONSD.&#x000a0;Consensual pupillary response assessment serves as an adjunct bedside neurologic measure.&#x000a0;Enlargement of the optic nerve sheath may indicate increased ICP. The examination is noninvasive and repeatable, allowing serial monitoring, particularly in individuals who are sedated or critically ill&#x000a0;and cannot participate in a neurologic examination.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r20">[20]</xref></p>
        <p>Vitreous hemorrhage appears on ultrasound as mobile, heterogeneous echoes within the vitreous chamber that swirl with eye movement, often described as the &#x0201c;snow globe&#x0201d; effect.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r21">[21]</xref> Retinal detachment appears on ultrasound as a mobile hyperechoic membrane tethered to the optic disc and moving with ocular motion. Retinal detachment may also occur in the setting of trauma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r23">[23]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-18081.s5" sec-type="Contraindications">
        <title>Contraindications</title>
        <p>Ocular ultrasound is contraindicated when globe rupture is suspected. The ultrasound should be discontinued if signs of globe rupture are identified during the examination.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-18081.s6" sec-type="Equipment">
        <title>Equipment</title>
        <p>Ocular ultrasound should be performed using a high-frequency linear probe on an ultrasound machine equipped with an ocular preset. Use of this setting limits acoustic output and reduces the risk of injury to the sensitive structures of the eye.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-18081.s7" sec-type="Personnel">
        <title>Personnel</title>
        <p>POCUS is inherently operator-dependent, as image acquisition, optimization, and interpretation rely on clinician skill and experience. Diagnostic accuracy may be inconsistent without structured training and supervised practice, particularly among novice users. Therefore, ocular ultrasound should be performed in accordance with established training and credentialing standards, including guidelines outlined by the American College of Emergency Physicians.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r24">[24]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-18081.s8" sec-type="Preparation">
        <title>Preparation</title>
        <p>Materials required for the examination include an ultrasound machine with a linear probe, ultrasound gel, and a clear plastic film, such as that manufactured under the brand name "Tegaderm." Sterile petroleum jelly or ophthalmic ointment may be applied to the lash line beneath the film but is not required. Use of sterile ultrasound gel or bacteriostatic single-use gel packets is recommended to minimize the risk of infection.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-18081.s9" sec-type="Technique or Treatment">
        <title>Technique or Treatment</title>
        <p>If desired, a small amount of ointment may be applied directly to the eyelid and lashes of a closed eye to minimize air trapping.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r25">[25]</xref> A clear plastic film is then applied directly to the closed eye, with care taken to avoid air trapping beneath the film. Air between the skin and the plastic film may create an artifact and interfere with the examination.</p>
        <p>A copious amount of ultrasound gel should be applied to the plastic film, with enough volume to allow the probe to rest lightly on the gel and minimize pressure on the eye. The provider&#x02019;s hand should rest lightly on the bridge of the patient&#x02019;s nose to stabilize the transducer. The orbit should be assessed in both sagittal and transverse planes.</p>
        <p>With the probe oriented horizontally, the globe should be scanned from top to bottom. With the probe maintained in a stationary horizontal position, the patient should look up and down continuously for several seconds. In the sagittal plane, the globe should be scanned from right to left. With the probe held stationary, the patient should look back and forth from right to left.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-18081.s10" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>With the exclusion of potential injury in the setting of globe rupture, ocular ultrasound carries minimal risk to the patient and, when performed correctly, should not cause significant discomfort. Excessive pressure applied to the eye may cause discomfort and, in rare cases, trigger a trigemino-vagal reflex known as the oculocardiac reflex. This reflex may result in nausea, bradycardia, or, in severe cases, cardiac arrest.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r26">[26]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r27">[27]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-18081.s11" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>Traditional methods of examining deeper ocular structures, such as dilated fundoscopic and slit-lamp examination, can be challenging and require significant experience before proficiency is achieved. Computed tomography and magnetic resonance imaging are noninvasive modalities used to evaluate the globe and orbit.&#x000a0;However, computed tomography is associated with ionizing radiation exposure, and both diagnostic tools can&#x000a0;cause delays in care and increased cost.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18081.r28">[28]</xref> Conventional ICP evaluation by emergency medicine physicians requires lumbar puncture, a time-consuming procedure that carries patient risk. In many cases, ultrasound provides an alternative to these technologies, offering advantages such as ease of use, low cost, the absence of ionizing radiation, and bedside availability.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-18081.s12" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>POCUS serves as a critical&#x000a0;bedside imaging modality for the rapid evaluation of patients presenting with acute vision loss, ocular trauma, headache, or suspected ICP increase. When direct visualization or fundoscopy is limited or not feasible, ocular ultrasound can identify vision- and life-threatening conditions, including retinal or vitreous detachment, vitreous hemorrhage, lens dislocation, IOFBs, retrobulbar hematoma, CRAO, and papilledema. Early recognition of these conditions can expedite appropriate management and consultation, improving diagnostic efficiency and patient outcomes. ONSD measurement provides a noninvasive method for&#x000a0;determining ICP elevation at the bedside, serving as a rapid adjunct to clinical assessment in patients with critical illness.</p>
        <p>Optimal use of ocular POCUS requires coordinated collaboration among emergency physicians, advanced practice providers, nurses, ophthalmologists, neurologists, and radiologists. Accurate bedside identification of ocular pathology supports timely consultation with specialists, such as ophthalmologists, and facilitates rapid activation of&#x000a0;interprofessional care pathways, including stroke teams in cases of suspected CRAO. Interprofessional collaboration also supports the development of standardized protocols for ocular ultrasound use, structured training programs, and competency assessments. Such efforts improve diagnostic consistency, enhance communication among team members, and promote timely, patient-centered care in emergency and critical care settings.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-18081.s13">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
