    <back>
      <floats-group id="article-18081.s14">
        <fig id="article-18081.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Anatomical Diagram and Sonographic&#x000a0;Image of the Eye. Panel A displays a medial sagittal illustration of the globe, labeling the internal chambers, retinal layers, and vascular supply. Panel B presents a corresponding B-scan ultrasound image, identifying sonographic landmarks such as the cornea, lens, and optic nerve shadow.&#x000a0;Optic nerve exit and retinal vasculature are emphasized as key posterior landmarks. Contributed by Harry J. Goett, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Ocular__Diagram__and__US__horizontalx1x" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-18081.s15">
        <fig id="article-18081.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Ultrasound of the Right Globe. This image demonstrates a transverse cross-section of the right eye with clear visualization of the anterior and posterior segments. Hyperechoic lens curvature and the posterior globe wall are evident against the anechoic vitreous chamber. Distal acoustic shadowing marks the position of the optic nerve head at the posterior pole. Contributed by Harry Goett, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Lens__dislocation" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-18081.s16">
        <fig id="article-18081.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Dynamic Ultrasonography of Retinal Detachment. The video demonstrates a thick, hyperechoic membrane within the posterior segment that remains tethered to the optic nerve head. The membrane exhibits restricted after-movement during oculokinetic echography, distinguishing this structure from the more fluid, undulating motion seen in a posterior vitreous detachment. The subretinal space appears anechoic, indicating the accumulation of fluid beneath the separated sensory retina. Contributed by Harry J. Goett, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <media xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Retinal__Detachment__x1x.mp4" mime-subtype="mp4" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-18081.s17">
        <fig id="article-18081.image.f4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Dynamic Ultrasonography of Vitreous Hemorrhage. This scan captures the motion of fine, hyperechoic specks that shift freely as the patient performs saccades. Lack of a high-reflectivity linear interface suggests the absence of retinal detachment. This kinetic examination helps differentiate shifting blood from more organized intraocular structures. Contributed by Harry J Goett, MD.</p>
          </caption>
          <media xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Vitreous__hemorrhage.mp4" mime-subtype="mp4" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-18081.s18">
        <fig id="article-18081.image.f5" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Central Retinal Artery Occlusion. Fundoscopic examination demonstrates a cherry-red fovea, optic disc pallor, and boxcar segmentation of the retinal veins. Contributed by EJ Kaufman, OD, FAAO</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="CRAO" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-18081.s19">
        <fig id="article-18081.image.f6" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Papilledema on Ultrasound. The left image demonstrates a transverse view of the optic nerve sheath with measurement indicating widening to 8.7 mm (normal typically &#x0003c;5 mm), consistent with elevated intracranial pressure. The right image demonstrates the optic nerve head with disc elevation measuring 1.3 mm, consistent with papilledema. Together, these ultrasound images demonstrate characteristic findings of increased intracranial pressure affecting the optic nerve. Contributed by Harry J. Goett, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="WONS__and__Papilledema" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-18081.s20">
        <fig id="article-18081.image.f7" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Papilledema on Funduscopy. This photograph demonstrates optic disc swelling secondary to increased intracranial pressure, characterized by blurred disc margins, elevation of the optic nerve head, and engorged retinal veins, which are hallmark findings of papilledema. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Papilledema#/media/File:Papilledema.jpg</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Papilledema" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
